\description{
\code{emeans()} clusters the rows of \code{x} into \code{k} groups, much as
\code{\link[stats:kmeans]{stats::kmeans()}} does, but learns from small random batches instead of
the whole data set at once. Each batch pulls its nearest centers part of
the way towards it and the pull weakens over a few passes, until a closing
pass puts every center at the mean of its points. The authors of e-means
report a lower loss than Lloyd's k-means in about a tenth of the passes
(Bessa et al. 2026). A final sweep then labels every row by its nearest
center, so the fit carries the same \code{cluster}, \code{size} and sums of squares
as a \code{\link[stats:kmeans]{stats::kmeans()}} fit.

Most fits need only \code{x} and \code{k}. The remaining arguments either adjust the
training recipe, which \code{emeans()} otherwise chooses from the size of the
data, or decide where and how fast the work runs.
}
\section{Devices}{

On \code{"cpu"}, emeans runs C++ code in double precision. Every other device
runs the same algorithm in float32 through \pkg{torch} and returns an
ordinary \code{"emeans"} object, and \link[=predict.emeans]{predict()} then assigns
new data on the same device. \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} still controls every random draw,
but the results differ slightly from the CPU because float32 rounds
differently, and on CUDA they can even differ from one run to the next.

The CUDA path has not been tested on NVIDIA hardware, so please report what
you find at \url{https://github.com/coatless-rpkg/emeans/issues}. A
\code{torch_tensor} passed as \code{x} is not checked for \code{NA}, \code{NaN} or infinite
values, since that would mean copying it back from the device.
}

\section{Speed}{

\code{threads} sets the number of CPU threads, up to any limit in the
\code{OMP_THREAD_LIMIT} environment variable. R for macOS is built without
OpenMP, so on a Mac emeans uses one thread.

On the CPU, emeans uses a BLAS-based kernel when R is linked to an
optimised BLAS and a simpler direct kernel otherwise. Set
\code{options(emeans.kernel = "direct")} when you need results that are
identical to the last bit whatever the chunk sizes.
}

\section{Differences from upstream}{

emeans ports the e-means code in faiss (\code{contrib/e_means}), with these
deliberate differences:
\enumerate{
\item R's random number generator and \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} replace numpy's
\code{RandomState} and the \code{seed} argument, so a run does not match Python
for the same seed.
\item The CPU path uses double precision throughout, where upstream uses
float32 for the model and float64 for the objective.
\item Cluster labels start at 1, and rows left out of training get \code{NA}
rather than \code{-1}.
\item A tie for the nearest center goes to the lowest index.
\item The split-collision step moves to the next representable double, where
upstream uses the next float32.
\item \code{emeans()} ends with the extra sweep that labels every row by its
nearest final center and adds the sums of squares. Upstream returns the
labels of the last training pass, which \code{\link[=em_accumulate]{em_accumulate()}} still gives.
}
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
x <- rbind(matrix(rnorm(200, sd = 0.3), ncol = 2),
           matrix(rnorm(200, mean = 3, sd = 0.3), ncol = 2))
fit <- emeans(x, 2, threads = 1)
fit$centers

# Assign new points to the nearest center
predict(fit, rbind(c(0, 0), c(3, 3)))
}
